# 4. BREAK ORDER

At the beginning of a match:

Each player picks one card from a standard 52-card deck.

Cards are initially face-down.

The highest card gets the first break.

Card ranking:

K > Q > J > 10 > 9 > 8 > 7 > 6 > 5 > 4 > 3 > 2 > A

Ace is ALWAYS the lowest rank.

If players draw the same rank, compare suits.

Suit ranking:

♠ Spades

>

♥ Hearts

>

♦ Diamonds

>

♣ Clubs

Examples:

A♠ beats A♥

K♦ beats K♣

After determining the first breaker, the remaining players shoot in descending order based on their drawn cards.

This order becomes the initial turn order.

The POV must rotate clockwise.

---

# 5. CARD DEALING

After break order is determined:

Each player receives exactly:

7 poker cards.

Use a standard 52-card deck.

Each card corresponds to a numbered billiard ball.

Mapping:

Ace → Ball 1
2 → Ball 2
3 → Ball 3
4 → Ball 4
5 → Ball 5
6 → Ball 6
7 → Ball 7
8 → Ball 8
9 → Ball 9
10 → Ball 10
Jack → Ball 11
Queen → Ball 12
King → Ball 13

This mapping is independent of suit.

Example:

A♠ = Ball 1

A♥ = Ball 1

K♣ = Ball 13

Q♦ = Ball 12

---

# 6. DUPLICATE CARD/RANK OBJECTIVE RULE

This is one of the MOST IMPORTANT rules.

Duplicate cards representing the SAME numbered ball require that ball to be pocketed ONLY ONCE.

Example:

A♠
A♥
A♦
A♣
7♥
7♦
7♣

Required objectives:

Ball 1
Ball 7

NOT seven separate objectives.

Another example:

2♠
8♥
5♦
5♣
7♥
Q♦
K♣

Required balls:

2
8
5
7
12
13

Therefore:

4 copies of Ace still require Ball 1 only once.

2 copies of 5 still require Ball 5 only once.

Implement this through a unique objective-ball set.

Do NOT create duplicate objective requirements for the same numbered ball.

---

# 7. CARD DISTRIBUTION RULE

No two players may receive the exact same physical card.

For example:

Only one player can receive:

A♠

Only one player can receive:

7♥

Only one player can receive:

K♦

However, different suits of the same rank may be distributed to different players.

For example:

Player 1 can have A♠

Player 2 can have A♥

Player 3 can have A♦

Player 4 can have A♣

This is valid.

Implement the dealing algorithm so that the same exact 52-card identity cannot be dealt to two players.

